ist for example is order n it's  slow as dirt  but there's a couple tricks here we can  use that can speed things up  considerably  with an array finding an item is the  bottleneck but if you already know the  index of an item  under certain conditions this can be  extremely fast  consider this array you could imagine  these are items in your world  for example and say here at this index  you want to remove this guy here  normally when you think of removing an  item from an array  you have to do a somewhat complicated  bit of surgery to the array  you take the item out then you go and  shift everything left by one so that  everything is back in the same order  no gaps or anything just missing the  item you wanted removed  but here's where you can make it faster  if you don't give a crap about the order  of the items  instead of shifting everything over you  can simply swap the item with the last  item in the list and then pop it off the  end  job done barely takes any time at all  similarly for a linked list  finding the item is the bottleneck but  given a node in a list  removing it is trivial let's use a  doubly linked list  as an example you've got your pointers  to the previous to next items in the  chain  so removing an item is as simple as  modifying the previous item in the chain  and pointing it past you to the next  item and then doing the same for the  next item in the chain just pointing it  to the one behind you  at that point the item you wanted  removed isn't referenced anywhere  effectively removing it from the list  back to the code  so how does this help us in the context  of this delete function  it means that if the client had carried  some extra information about exactly  where it was in the list for any given  cell  this delete function would be trivial  you might be able to get an  array to work here but i'm going to go  with a doubly linked list for simplicity  so what we'll do is we'll need to make a  handful of small changes throughout the  