 a little  involved but if you're familiar with  linked lists there should have been a  breeze  let's test the performance out so i  won't bother with a profile and i'll  just reload and let's check out the  numbers  the first thing that pops out is the  find near calls appear to be slightly  slower but that's not too unexpected  the major difference in find nearby is  the use of a linked list instead of the  built-in set type so i could believe  there's some heavy duty optimization on  v8's end of things to make the set  container as fast as possible  faster than iterating our own hand  rolled link list  the real changes come from the insert  delete functions that's quite a bit  faster  the time difference here is massive  around two and a half to three times  faster so this is really substantial  depending on the game and the usage  patterns this trade-off is probably  easily worth it and there's still room  for more improvement and easy wins in  there  back in the code let's scroll over to  the update function  this is implemented in a super simple  way we simply remove the item from the  grid  and then we reinsert it now you can try  and do something more complicated like  figure out which indices it used to be  in  and which ones it should be in now and  do a difference on the two  but let's think about the problem a bit  let's start with a basic rectangular  world so i'll just draw a super crappy  one  and then let's just throw some random  crap in there real quick so i'll add  some people and monsters and whatever  else  you can just endure me drawing some  stuff so if i overlay a grid on top  and now let's think about what's  happening in any given frame  the game is likely to be running at  something like 30 fps  minimum meaning you get about 1 30th of  a second to do anything like movement  and whatever else  now even if you're using small cells 1  30th of a second  isn't a whole lot of time if this guy is  walking across the field  they'll barely make it 